<Doc
  title="When should I talk to a GP about snoring?"
  description="The signs that mean it is worth talking to a GP about snoring, and what SnoreHive can and cannot tell you."
  eyebrow="Questions people ask"
  lede="Snoring is common. Some signs mean it is worth talking to a GP about it."
>
  <h2>Talk to your GP if</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>someone has seen you stop breathing in your sleep;</li>
    <li>you gasp, or wake up choking;</li>
    <li>you feel very sleepy in the day;</li>
    <li>your nose is blocked most of the time, or congestion lasts for more than a few weeks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What SnoreHive can and cannot do</h2>
  <p>
    SnoreHive measures snoring and shows patterns: how much of the night you snored, how loud it was, and what changed it. It does
    not diagnose any condition, so a quiet score is not a reason to ignore the signs above.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking your nights to an appointment</h2>
  <p>
    Hive members can make a snoring summary for their doctor: the snore score for each night, how loud the snoring was, what they
    logged and tried, and oxygen readings from a smartwatch if they connected one. It gives the appointment real nights to start
    from.
  </p>
</Doc>
